## COMMUNICATION STYLE

When writing code:
- **Be explicit about data quality.** Comment on known gaps, not to apologise for them, but to document them.
- **Make assumptions visible.** If you've chosen a default (e.g., "assume 30-day rolling window"), document why.
- **Link back to the research.** In docstrings, reference the chapters or research reports that justify a design choice.
- **Acknowledge limitations.** "This model achieves X accuracy on the training set; the EPC performance-gap literature suggests it will be less accurate on real energy use" is better than silent overconfidence.

When you get stuck:
- **Check the Master Project Instructions and CURSOR_BUILD_SPEC.md first.** The decision may already be made.
- **If you find yourself redesigning something (e.g., "we should use FastAPI instead of Streamlit"), pause and flag it to RJ rather than implementing the change unilaterally.**
- **If you're adding a feature not in the spec, ask first.**

